\section{Stress Sovereignty Corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Post-Sovereign Stability Corollary]
Let $\mathcal{S}$ denote the unified multi-domain simulation stack and
$\mathcal{I}$ the invariant stability set as defined in the Stress
Sovereignty Theorem. If $\mathcal{S}$ achieves stress sovereignty under
maximum-stress load, then for any subsequent stress load
$\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{future}}$ satisfying:
\[
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{future}} \leq \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{max}},
\]
the system remains globally stable, drift-free, and invariant-preserving
without requiring additional synchronization or recalibration.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
From the Stress Sovereignty Theorem, maximum-stress load implies:
\[
\text{Integrity} = 100\%, \qquad
\text{Convergence} = \text{Absolute}.
\]
Thus, the unified architecture maintains:
\[
\Xi_{\mathrm{dual}} \ \text{locked}, \quad
\sum F_{\mathrm{net}} \to 0, \quad
\frac{d[\mathrm{Aggregates}]}{dt} < 0,
\]
\[
F_{\mathrm{synapse}} \approx 1, \quad
\Delta \tau\ \text{stable}, \quad
\delta_{\mathrm{smd}} \approx 1.
\]

Because each invariant remains bounded and drift-free at maximum load,
any future load $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{future}}$ satisfying
$\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{future}} \leq \mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{max}}$ cannot
exceed the proven stability envelope. Therefore, all invariants in
$\mathcal{I}$ remain preserved, and the system retains global stability
without requiring additional synchronization.

Hence, post-sovereign stability is guaranteed.
\end{proof}